            'The first is the <strong>future perfect</strong>: <strong>will have</strong> + past participle. It describes something that will be finished before a moment in the future: &ldquo;By the end of March, the ministry <strong>will have published</strong> its draft.&rdquo; Put a name and a date in the sentence and it becomes an <strong>action item</strong>. Notice the time word: <strong>by</strong> March, not until March, and <strong>by the time</strong> we meet, not by the time we will meet.',
